IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893, Part 2 "Proof": sections 58 to 80B.
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 2020 to 2723.
--
-- The questions this module answers: WHAT NEED NOT BE PROVED (58 to 60), HOW A FACT IS PROVED BY
-- ORAL EVIDENCE (61 to 62A), and HOW THE CONTENTS OF A DOCUMENT ARE PROVED (63 to 80B): primary and
-- secondary evidence, notice to produce, signatures and attestation, public documents and certified
-- copies.  Electronic records are documents here (section 3(1) "document" (d) and (e), "electronic
-- record") and are addressed by the Explanations to section 64 and by section 116A in
-- ea-presumptions-burden.l4.

§ `58 to 60. Facts which need not be proved`

-- 59(1): the court is to take judicial notice of the facts in paragraphs (a) to (n): (a) laws of
-- Singapore; (b) Acts of Commonwealth legislatures; (c) articles of war; (d) the course of proceedings
-- of Parliament and Commonwealth legislatures; (e) the election of the President; (f) public and other
-- seals; (g) the appointment, names, titles, functions and signatures of gazetted public officers;
-- (h) the existence, title and national flag of recognised States; (i) the ordinary course of nature,
-- divisions of time, geographical divisions, the meaning of English words, gazetted public festivals,
-- fasts and holidays; (j) the countries in the Commonwealth; (k) the commencement, continuance and
-- termination of hostilities; (l) the names of the members and officers of the court and of those
-- authorised to appear before it; (m) the rule of the road; (n) all other matters a written law
-- directs the court to notice.  The fourteen are one class for the purposes of section 58 and are
-- one field in `Judicial Notice Facts`.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 58 and 59(1)"
GIVEN j IS A `Judicial Notice Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fact need not be proved because the court takes judicial notice of it` IF
    j's `the fact is within section 59(1)(a) to (n)`

-- 59(2) and (3): the court may resort for its aid to books or documents of reference, in all those
-- cases and on matters of public history, literature, science or art; and if called on to take
-- judicial notice of a fact, may refuse unless the person produces the book or document the court
-- considers necessary.  The refusal is a discretion.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 59(2) and (3)"
GIVEN j IS A `Judicial Notice Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may resort to books or documents of reference` IF
        j's `the fact is within section 59(1)(a) to (n)`
    OR  j's `the fact is a matter of public history, literature, science or art`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 60(1) and (2)"
GIVEN j IS A `Judicial Notice Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fact need not be proved because it is admitted` IF
        (   j's `the parties or their agents agree to admit the fact at the hearing`
         OR j's `the parties agree before the hearing, in a writing under their hands, to admit the fact`
         OR j's `the parties are by a rule of pleading in force deemed to have admitted the fact by their pleadings`)
    AND NOT j's `the court requires the admitted fact to be proved otherwise than by the admission`

§ `61 to 62A. Oral evidence`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 61"
GIVEN o IS AN `Oral Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fact may be proved by oral evidence` IF
    NOT o's `the evidence is of the contents of a document`

-- 62(2): the opinions of experts expressed in a treatise commonly offered for sale, and the grounds,
-- may be proved by production of the treatise if the author is dead, cannot be found, has become
-- incapable of giving evidence, or cannot be called without delay or expense the court regards as
-- unreasonable.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 62(2)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Oral Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion may be proved by production of the treatise under section 62(2)` IF
        o's `the opinion is expressed in a treatise commonly offered for sale`
    AND o's `the author of the treatise is dead, cannot be found, has become incapable of giving evidence, or cannot be called without an amount of delay or expense the court regards as unreasonable`

-- 62(1): oral evidence must in all cases be direct: if it refers to a fact that could be seen, it must
-- be the evidence of a witness who says he or she saw it; heard, heard it; perceived by another sense,
-- perceived it so; and if it refers to an opinion or its grounds, the evidence of the person who holds
-- the opinion on those grounds (subject to 62(2)).  This is the Act's rule against second-hand oral
-- evidence of what a witness did not perceive; the exceptions for what others said are in section 32.
-- 62(3) (the court may require production of a material thing) is a discretion and is inert here.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 62(1)(a) to (d)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Oral Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the oral evidence is direct under section 62(1)` IF
        (   NOT o's `the evidence refers to a fact which could be seen`
         OR o's `the witness says the witness saw that fact`)
    AND (   NOT o's `the evidence refers to a fact which could be heard`
         OR o's `the witness says the witness heard that fact`)
    AND (   NOT o's `the evidence refers to a fact which could be perceived by another sense or in another manner`
         OR o's `the witness says the witness perceived that fact by that sense or in that manner`)
    AND (   NOT o's `the evidence refers to an opinion or to the grounds on which it is held`
         OR o's `the evidence is that of the person who holds the opinion on those grounds`
         OR `the opinion may be proved by production of the treatise under section 62(2)` o)

-- 62A(1): despite any other provision of the Act, a person may, with the court's permission, give
-- evidence through a live video or live television link in any proceedings OTHER THAN proceedings in
-- a criminal matter or for contempt of court, if (a) the witness is below 18, (b) the parties expressly
-- agree, (c) the witness is outside Singapore, or (d) the court is satisfied it is expedient in the
-- interests of justice.  For criminal proceedings the equivalent is Criminal Procedure Code s 281.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 62A(1)(a) to (d)"
GIVEN v IS A `Video Link Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence may be given through a live video or live television link under section 62A(1)` IF
        NOT v's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
    AND NOT v's `the proceedings are proceedings for contempt of court`
    AND v's `the court gives permission`
    AND (   v's `the witness is below 18 years of age`
         OR v's `it is expressly agreed between the parties that evidence may be so given`
         OR v's `the witness is outside Singapore`
         OR v's `the court is satisfied that it is expedient in the interests of justice`)

-- 62A(6A): in civil proceedings, with the express agreement of all the parties under the statutory
-- provisions named, the parties may apply for permission for a witness to give evidence through a live
-- audio link only; the application must describe the arrangements agreed to ensure integrity and
-- quality.  The Act states what the application must contain and that the court grants permission;
-- it does not state the test for the court's decision, which is not encoded.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 62A(6A)"
GIVEN v IS A `Video Link Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence may be given through a live audio link only under section 62A(6A)` IF
        v's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `civil proceedings`
    AND v's `all the parties to the civil proceedings expressly agree under section 11(3)(b) of the Family Justice Act 2014, section 8(3)(b) of the State Courts Act 1970 or section 8A(3)(b) of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969`
    AND v's `the application describes the arrangements the parties have agreed to ensure the integrity and quality of the evidence`
    AND v's `the court gives permission`

-- 62A(7) and (8): evidence so given is deemed to have been given in the proceedings for the purposes of
-- sections 193, 194, 195, 196 and 205 of the Penal Code 1871 (false evidence), and the witness is
-- deemed to be giving evidence in the presence of the court.  Both deemings follow whenever evidence is
-- given "by virtue of this section" and so apply wherever one of the two rules above is met.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 62A(7) and (8)"
GIVEN v IS A `Video Link Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness is deemed to give evidence in the presence of the court and in the proceedings` IF
        `evidence may be given through a live video or live television link under section 62A(1)` v
    OR  `evidence may be given through a live audio link only under section 62A(6A)` v

§ `63 to 68. Primary and secondary evidence of the contents of documents`

-- 63: the contents of documents may be proved by primary or by secondary evidence.
-- 64: primary evidence means the document itself produced for the inspection of the court.
-- Explanation 1: each part of a document executed in parts is primary evidence of the document; each
-- counterpart is primary evidence as against the parties executing it.  Explanation 2: documents all
-- made by one uniform process (printing, lithography, photography) are each primary evidence of the
-- contents of the rest, but copies of a common original are not primary evidence of the original.
-- Explanation 3: despite Explanation 2, a copy of a document in the form of an electronic record shown
-- to reflect the document accurately is primary evidence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 64 and Explanations 1 to 3"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the thing produced is primary evidence of the document` IF
        d's `the document itself is produced for the inspection of the court`
    OR  d's `the document was executed in several parts and the thing produced is one of the parts`
    OR  d's `the document was executed in counterpart and the thing produced is a counterpart offered against a party who executed it`
    OR  d's `the thing produced is a copy of a document in the form of an electronic record and is shown to reflect that document accurately`
    OR  (   d's `the thing produced is one of a number of documents all made by one uniform process, offered as evidence of the contents of another of them`
        AND NOT d's `the thing produced and the document it is offered to prove are copies of a common original`)

-- 65: secondary evidence means and includes (a) certified copies given under the Act; (b) except for
-- copies referred to in Explanation 3 to section 64, copies made from the original by technical
-- processes which in themselves ensure the accuracy of the copy, and copies compared with such copies;
-- (c) copies made from or compared with the original; (d) counterparts as against parties who did not
-- execute them; (e) oral accounts of the contents given by a person who has himself or herself seen it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 65(a) to (e)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the thing produced is secondary evidence of the document` IF
        d's `the thing produced is a certified copy given under the provisions of the Act`
    OR  (   d's `the thing produced is a copy made from the original by a technical process that in itself ensures the accuracy of the copy, or a copy compared with such a copy`
        AND NOT d's `the thing produced is a copy of a document in the form of an electronic record and is shown to reflect that document accurately`)
    OR  d's `the thing produced is a copy made from or compared with the original`
    OR  d's `the thing produced is a counterpart offered against a party who did not execute it`
    OR  d's `the thing produced is an oral account of the contents given by a person who has himself or herself seen the document`

-- 68(1): secondary evidence of the contents of the documents in section 67(1)(a) shall not be given
-- unless the party proposing to give it has previously given the party in possession, or that party's
-- solicitor, the notice to produce prescribed by law or, if none is prescribed, such notice as the court
-- considers reasonable.  68(2): the notice is not required where (a) the document is itself a notice;
-- (b) the adverse party must know it will be required to produce it; (c) the adverse party obtained
-- possession of the original by fraud or force; (d) the adverse party or the adverse party's agent has
-- the original in court; (e) the adverse party or agent has admitted the loss; (f) the person in
-- possession is out of reach of or not subject to the process of the court; or in any other case in
-- which the court thinks fit to dispense with it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 68(1) and (2)(a) to (f)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notice to produce required by section 68 is given or dispensed with` IF
        d's `the party proposing to give secondary evidence has previously given the party in possession, or that party's solicitor, the notice to produce prescribed by law or, if none is prescribed, the notice the court considers reasonable`
    OR  d's `the document to be proved is itself a notice`
    OR  d's `from the nature of the case the adverse party must know that the adverse party will be required to produce it`
    OR  d's `it appears or is proved that the adverse party has obtained possession of the original by fraud or force`
    OR  d's `the adverse party or the adverse party's agent has the original in court`
    OR  d's `the adverse party or the adverse party's agent has admitted the loss of the document`
    OR  d's `the person in possession of the document is out of reach of or not subject to the process of the court`
    OR  d's `the court thinks fit to dispense with the notice`

-- 67(1)(a) to (g): the seven cases in which secondary evidence may be given of the existence, condition
-- or contents of a document admissible in evidence.  Each case is a rule of its own because section
-- 67(2) to (5) say what kind of secondary evidence each opens.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(a)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(a)` IF
        (   d's `the original is shown or appears to be in the possession or power of the person against whom the document is sought to be proved`
         OR d's `the original is shown or appears to be in the possession or power of a person out of reach of or not subject to the process of the court`
         OR d's `the original is shown or appears to be in the possession or power of a person legally bound to produce it`)
    AND d's `after the notice mentioned in section 68 that person does not produce it`
    AND `the notice to produce required by section 68 is given or dispensed with` d

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(b)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(b)` IF
    d's `the existence, condition or contents of the original have been proved to be admitted in writing by the person against whom it is proved or by that person's representative in interest`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(c)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(c)` IF
        d's `the original has been destroyed or lost`
    OR  d's `the party offering evidence of its contents cannot, for a reason not arising from the party's own default or neglect, produce it in reasonable time`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(d)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(d)` IF
    d's `the original is of such a nature as not to be easily movable`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(e)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(e)` IF
    d's `the original is a public document within the meaning of section 76`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(f)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(f)` IF
    d's `the original is a document of which a certified copy is permitted by the Act or by another law in force in Singapore to be given in evidence`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(1)(g)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(g)` IF
    d's `the originals consist of numerous accounts or other documents that cannot conveniently be examined in court and the fact to be proved is the general result of the whole collection`

-- 66: documents must be proved by primary evidence except in the cases in section 67.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 66 and 67(1)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `secondary evidence may be given of the document` IF
        `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(a)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(b)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(c)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(d)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(e)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(f)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(g)` d

-- 67(2) to (5): what the cases admit.  (2) in cases (a), (c) and (d), any secondary evidence of the
-- contents; (3) in case (b), the written admission; (4) in case (e) or (f), a certified copy but no
-- other kind; (5) in case (g), evidence of the general result by a skilled person.  Where more than one
-- case applies the party has the benefit of each, so these are separate answers and not one ranking.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(2)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `any secondary evidence of the contents is admissible` IF
        `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(a)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(c)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(d)` d

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(3)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the written admission is admissible in proof of the contents` IF
    `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(b)` d

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(4)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a certified copy is admissible in proof of the contents` IF
        `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(e)` d
    OR  `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(f)` d
    OR  `any secondary evidence of the contents is admissible` d

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67(5)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of the general result by a skilled person is admissible` IF
    `secondary evidence may be given under section 67(1)(g)` d

-- 69: if a document is alleged to be signed or written wholly or in part by a person, the signature or
-- handwriting must be proved to be that person's; but not for an electronic record or electronic
-- signature to which the Electronic Transactions Act 2010 applies.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 69(1) and (2)"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signature or handwriting must be proved under section 69` IF
        d's `the document is alleged to be signed or written wholly or in part by a person`
    AND NOT d's `the document is an electronic record or electronic signature to which the Electronic Transactions Act 2010 applies`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 69"
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 69 is satisfied` IF
        NOT `the signature or handwriting must be proved under section 69` d
    OR  d's `the signature or handwriting has been proved to be in that person's handwriting`

§ `67A and 68A. Statements in documents; summaries of voluminous evidence`

-- 67A: where a statement in a document is admissible by virtue of section 32(1), it may be proved by
-- production of the document or (whether or not the document is still in existence) by production of
-- a copy of the document, or of the material part of it, authenticated in a manner approved by the court.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 67A"
GIVEN s IS A `Document Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement in the document is properly proved under section 67A` IF
        s's `a statement in a document is admissible in evidence by virtue of section 32(1)`
    AND (   s's `the statement is proved by production of the document`
         OR s's `the statement is proved by production of a copy of the document, or of the material part of it, authenticated in a manner approved by the court`)

-- 68A(1): evidence may be given in the form of charts, summaries or other explanatory material if it
-- appears to the court that (a) the materials would aid comprehension of other relevant and admissible
-- evidence and (b) the evidence is so voluminous or complex that the court considers it convenient to
-- assess it by reference to them.  68A(2): any fact or opinion asserted in the materials must be proved
-- by relevant and admissible evidence, unless the party undertakes to prove it and the court is
-- satisfied.  68A(3): the court's powers to direct provision of the materials are inert here.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 68A(1) and (2)"
GIVEN s IS A `Document Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence may be given in the form of charts, summaries or other explanatory material` IF
        s's `the materials would be likely to aid the court's comprehension of other evidence that is relevant and admissible`
    AND s's `the evidence to be given by a party is so voluminous or complex that the court considers it convenient to assess the evidence by reference to such materials`
    AND s's `every fact or opinion asserted in the materials is proved by relevant and admissible evidence, or the party undertakes to prove it and the court is satisfied with the undertaking`

§ `70 to 75. Attestation, signatures and comparison`

-- 70: a document required by law to be attested shall not be used as evidence until one attesting
-- witness at least has been called to prove execution, if there is an attesting witness alive, subject to
-- the process of the court and capable of giving evidence.  Where there is none, section 71 applies, and
-- the document may then be used only on the proof it describes: so that there is no attesting witness alive
-- is not itself enough (the field `there is an attesting witness alive ...` is therefore not read by the rule;
-- `no attesting witness can be found` is).  71: if no attesting witness can be found, or
-- if the document purports to have been executed in the United Kingdom, it must be proved that the
-- attestation of one attesting witness at least is in his or her handwriting and that the signature of
-- the person executing it is in that person's handwriting.  72: a party's admission of execution is
-- sufficient proof as against that party, though the document is required by law to be attested.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 70, 71 and 72"
GIVEN a IS AN `Attestation Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the document required to be attested may be used as evidence` IF
        NOT a's `the document is required by law to be attested`
    OR  a's `at least one attesting witness has been called to prove its execution`
    OR  a's `a party to the attested document has admitted its execution by himself or herself`
    OR  (   (   a's `no attesting witness can be found`
             OR a's `the document purports to have been executed in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland`)
        AND a's `it is proved that the attestation of at least one attesting witness is in that witness's handwriting and that the signature of the person executing the document is in that person's handwriting`)

-- 73: if the attesting witness denies or does not recollect the execution, it may be proved by other
-- evidence.  74: an attested document not required by law to be attested may be proved as if unattested.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 73"
GIVEN a IS AN `Attestation Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `execution may be proved by other evidence under section 73` IF
    a's `the attesting witness denies or does not recollect the execution of the document`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 74"
GIVEN a IS AN `Attestation Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the document may be proved as if it were unattested` IF
    NOT a's `the document is required by law to be attested`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 75(1)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Attestation Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signature, writing or seal may be compared under section 75` IF
    a's `the signature, writing or seal to be proved is compared with one admitted or proved to the satisfaction of the court to have been written or made by the person it purports to be by`

§ `76 to 80B. Public documents and certified copies`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 76(a) and (b)"
GIVEN p IS A `Public Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the document is a public document` IF
        p's `the document forms the acts or records of the acts of the sovereign authority`
    OR  p's `the document forms the acts or records of the acts of an official body or tribunal`
    OR  p's `the document forms the acts or records of the acts of a public officer, legislative, judicial or executive, of Singapore, of a part of the Commonwealth or of a foreign country`
    OR  p's `the document is a public record kept in Singapore of a private document`

-- 77: all other documents are private.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 77"
GIVEN p IS A `Public Document Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the document is a private document` IF
    NOT `the document is a public document` p

-- 78(1) and (2): a public officer having custody of a public document that any person has a right to
-- inspect must give the person on demand, on payment of the legal fees, a copy with a certificate written
-- at its foot that it is a true copy; the certificate must be dated and subscribed by the officer with the
-- officer's name and official title, and sealed whenever the officer is authorised by law to use a seal.
-- Such copies are "certified copies".  The Explanation deems an officer authorised in the ordinary course
-- of official duty to deliver such copies to have the custody of the documents.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 78(1) and (2) and Explanation"
GIVEN c IS A `Certified Copy Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the copy is a certified copy under section 78` IF
        c's `the officer has the custody of the document or is authorised in the ordinary course of official duty to deliver such copies`
    AND c's `the copy bears, written at its foot, a certificate that it is a true copy of the document or part of it`
    AND c's `the certificate is dated and subscribed by the officer with the officer's name and official title`
    AND (   NOT c's `the officer is authorised by law to make use of a seal`
         OR c's `the officer has sealed the certificate`)

-- 78(1): the officer's duty to give the copy on demand.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 78(1)"
GIVEN c IS A `Certified Copy Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer must give the person a certified copy on demand` IF
        c's `the person asking has a right to inspect the public document`
    AND c's `the person asks on demand and pays the legal fees`
    AND c's `the officer has the custody of the document or is authorised in the ordinary course of official duty to deliver such copies`

-- 79: such certified copies may be produced in proof of the contents of the public documents or parts of
-- them of which they purport to be copies.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 79"
GIVEN c IS A `Certified Copy Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the certified copy may be produced in proof of the contents of the public document` IF
    `the copy is a certified copy under section 78` c

-- 80(1)(a) to (g): how each of seven classes of public document may be proved.  The list is a table
-- of the permitted modes by class; a class may be provable in more than one way ("or").
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 80(1)(a) to (g)"
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of Official Document`, m IS A `Mode of Proof of an Official Document`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the official document may be proved by` MEANS
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `an Act, order or notification of the Government in a department` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `the records of the department certified by its head or by a Minister` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `a document purporting to be printed or published by the authority of the Government` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a proceeding of Parliament` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `the minutes of Parliament` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `published Acts or abstracts` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `copies purporting to be printed or published by the authority of the Government` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `an Act, order or notification of the Government of Malaysia, or a proceeding of the Parliament of Malaysia` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `copies or extracts contained in the Government Gazette of Malaysia` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `the recognition of the Act, order, notification or proceeding in an Act or Ordinance of Malaysia or of a State of Malaysia` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a proclamation, order or regulation issued by Her Majesty, the Privy Council or a department of Her Majesty's Government` THEN
        m EQUALS `copies or extracts contained in the London Gazette or the Gazette`
    WHEN `an Act of the Executive or a proceeding of the legislature of a foreign country` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `journals published by authority of the foreign country or commonly received in that country as such` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `a copy certified under the seal of the country or sovereign` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `the recognition of the Act or proceeding in a public Act of Singapore` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a proceeding of a municipal body, town board or other local authority in Malaysia` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `a copy of the proceedings certified by the legal keeper` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `a printed book purporting to be published by the authority of the body` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a public document of any other class in a foreign country` THEN
        BRANCH IF m EQUALS `the original` THEN TRUE
               IF m EQUALS `a copy certified by the legal keeper with a certificate under the seal of a notary public or of a consular officer of Singapore that it is duly certified by the officer having legal custody of the original, upon proof of the character of the document according to the law of the foreign country` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE

-- 80(2) and (3): copies of Acts, Ordinances and Statutes of any country in the Commonwealth, and of
-- instruments made under them, purporting to be printed by the Government Printer, are received in
-- evidence by all courts in Singapore without proof that they were so printed.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 80(2) and (3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Commonwealth Statute Copy Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the copy is received in evidence without proof that it was printed by the Government Printer` IF
        c's `the copy is of an Act, Ordinance or Statute passed by the legislature of a country in the Commonwealth, or of an order, regulation or other instrument made under its authority`
    AND c's `the copy purports to be printed by the Government Printer of that country`

-- 80A(1): a print, whether enlarged or not, purporting to be made from a film of a document in the
-- possession of the Government or a specified statutory body may be produced in proof of the contents of
-- the document (or the part it purports to copy) upon proof that (a) while the document was in the custody
-- or control of the Government or body the film was taken to keep a permanent record, and (b) the
-- document was subsequently destroyed, so damaged as to be wholly or partly indecipherable, lost, or had
-- passed out of the custody or control of the Government or body.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 80A(1)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN f IS A `Film Print Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the print may be produced in proof of the contents under section 80A(1)` IF
        f's `the print purports to be made from a film of a document in the possession of the Government or a specified statutory body`
    AND f's `while the document was in the custody or control of the Government or the specified statutory body the film was taken in order to keep a permanent record`
    AND (   f's `the document photographed was subsequently destroyed`
         OR f's `the document photographed was so damaged as to be wholly or partly indecipherable`
         OR f's `the document photographed was lost`
         OR f's `the document photographed had passed out of the custody or control of the Government or the specified statutory body`)

-- 80A(3) and (4): a certificate under (2) is admissible on its production without further proof, and the
-- court is to presume, until the contrary is proved, that the facts stated in it relating to the print and
-- the compliance with (1) are true and that it was signed by the public officer or employee it purports to
-- be signed by.  A "specified statutory body" is one in the Second Schedule: see ea-schedules.l4.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 80A(2) to (5)"
GIVEN f IS A `Film Print Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the certificate under section 80A(2) is admissible and the court is to presume its contents` IF
        f's `a certificate under section 80A(2) purports to be signed by a public officer or an employee of the specified statutory body having custody or control of the film`
    AND f's `the body is a statutory body specified in the Second Schedule`

-- 80B: to avoid doubt, sections 76 to 80A do not affect the operation of Part 2 of the Apostille Act 2020.
-- That is a statement of non-effect and is inert here; the Apostille Act 2020 is not encoded.
